From 83951fcf7af011e09d9842348a6a4c8a3368ada0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: colorburst <dev@colorburst.invalid>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 23:05:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Fix three more places that a device-local account falls
 through

Found by booting the previous commit and walking the OOBE flow. Each is the
same upstream shape: a check that means "is this a normal human user" is
written as "does this user have a Gaia account", which stopped being the same
question once a local account existed.

1. wizard_controller.cc: OnLocalAccountScreenExit called a function that does
   not exist. Use AdvanceToScreen(UserCreationView::kScreenId).

2. session_manager.cc: SetIsCurrentUserNew() ANDed `new_user` with
   HasGaiaAccount(), so a brand new local user was not considered new, no
   onboarding ran, and UserSessionManager backfilled
   onboarding_completed_version = "0.0.0.0". The observable result was a
   session on the desktop whose cryptohome had NO auth_factors directory at
   all -- an account with no password, which the next boot would have
   discarded. Ask the type instead, which is what the guest/kiosk exclusion
   here actually means.

3. recovery_eligibility_screen.cc: a local account was silently opted in to
   cryptohome recovery, which wraps the recovery secret against a
   Google-hosted service and is redeemed by reauthenticating with Gaia.
   RecoveryFactorEditor then failed with code 50 and
   CryptohomeRecoverySetupScreen dead-ended on its error page, stranding the
   user in OOBE before the password screen. Note the opt-in is a default that
   is only *confirmed* later on ConsolidatedConsentScreen, which does not run
   for a Gaia-less user -- so it was both unconsented and unfulfillable.

4. known_user.cc: KnownUser::GetAccountId() never consulted the persisted
   account_type. The login screen's user list (prefs::kRegularUsersPref) is a
   flat list of e-mail strings, so UserManagerImpl::ParseUserList()
   reconstructs every AccountId with AccountType::UNKNOWN; a Google account
   survives because its gaia id is stored and recovered, but a local account
   has no id and would silently come back as UNKNOWN, so the LOCAL type would
   only ever exist during the session that created it.

Verified on a VM: the OOBE card -> the new screen -> a name -> upstream's
PasswordSelectionScreen forcing LOCAL_PASSWORD_FORCED ->
LocalPasswordSetupScreen -> auth_factors/password.local-password -> a desktop
session -> reboot -> the pod on the login screen -> the password typed on a
synthetic uinput keyboard -> a second session. No test API was used to create
or authenticate the account.
